er our partner has the competence and network to keep our project out of the firing line.” Then present the partner’s risk management capacity as the thing you’re actually betting on: | What triggers the risk | How the partner manages it | |------------------------|----------------------------| | Elections amplify ethnic rhetoric and party-client networks | Partner maintains strict non-partisanship; staff code of conduct prohibits political activity; programme beneficiaries are selected openly, not through party channels | | Local disputes over resources or land escalate into ethnic violence | Partner’s field staff are from the communities themselves; early warning and mediation roles are part of their model; they’ve resolved 20+ local conflicts in the last three years without project interruption | | A new administration might view our partner as aligned with the old regime | Partner has a visible multi-stakeholder board and a public track record of working with whoever is in power; they maintain relationships across the aisle as a matter of survival | ### 4. Be honest about what you *can’t* control—and offer the board the option to walk away End with a clear-eyed statement: > “There is no guarantee that ethnic political tensions won’t produce a future crisis that affects our work. That risk never drops to zero. What I can tell you is that we’ve stress-tested this partner against the last three episodes of political tension, and each time they adapted and kept operations going. I’m not asking us to ignore the pattern. I’m asking us to judge whether we’re willing to manage that risk with this particular organisation, or whether our appetite for political risk in any ethnically-mobilised environment is zero. If it’s zero, we should be consistent and apply that principle globally, not just to Sierra Leone.” This respects the board’s scepticism. It doesn’t label them as biased. It gives them a principled way to either greenlight with eyes open, or reject on risk appeti